WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.189 --> 00:00:06.679
Hypothetical question for you: what if frogs
ruled the Earth?

00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:12.360
Not just terms in terms of numbers… but
in terms of their position in the ecosystem.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:18.060
Amphiterra is a speculative biology project
by the talented artist and writer Roxy Valdez,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.270
which imagines the incredible and surreal
forms frogs might achieve if given the perfect

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.960
environment to evolve.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.070
While the world of Amphiterra often looks
like an alien planet, the artist imagines

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:34.989
it’s actually an alternate timeline of Earth,
where subtly different climatic conditions

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:40.250
led to early amphibians out competing the
first dinosaurs and protomammals.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.719
What creatures will develop and thrive in
this drastically different history?

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:50.079
For this entry into the archive, we’ll explore
the fascinating possibilities of the Amphiterra

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:51.079
Project.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:55.559
And remember to support Roxy Valdez on Patreon
using the links below if you’re a fan of

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:56.559
this project.

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:06.390
Now let’s leap back 252 million years, and
begin our tour through the timeline of Amphiterra...

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.560
Our epic tale of frog ascendency begins in
the early Triassic, with the oldest member

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.670
of the frog lineage: the diminutive Triadobatrachus.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:21.410
This amphibian is present in our timeline
as well, but in the alternate world of Amphiterra,

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:27.030
the wetter, damper conditions will lead Triadobatrachus
to be better suited for the period than early

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.100
synapsids and reptiles.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.880
As a result, its descendants will rise to
the top of this primordial Earth...

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:41.060
By the Late Triassic in what is now Nigeria,
the Common Eofrog has emerged from the Triadobatrachus

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.520
— and represents the first major deviation
from our world.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.220
Brought on by the ideal conditions of this
alternate timeline, the Eofrog is, essentially,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.530
just a big frog.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.380
But over many eons, it will provide the anatomical
basis the most significant species of this

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.450
alternate reality.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.430
The reign of the amphibians has now begun…

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.200
By the early Jurassic, the descendants of
the Eofrog have evolved to fill various major

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.560
niches across the ecosystems of the world.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.950
In the marshy plains, an effective early predator
has emerged in the form of the amusingly named

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.760
Greater Moistboy.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:23.940
While these lifeforms have a comical appearance,
the advance they represent is tremendous,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:29.040
having brought their limbs underneath their
body to be taller and faster than their competition.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:35.620
The Moistboy also possess an enlarged clavicular
ring, which essentially functions like a ribcage

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.940
and helps support their growing weight.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:43.370
Many of the most successful predators of the
future will branch off from this design.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:48.720
In the rainforests of what is now Northern
Africa, the Marbled Snapapple perches on a

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.720
branch.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:55.040
This early tree-dweller has evolved a creeping,
unpredictable form of movement convergent

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.430
with our timeline’s chameleons, and will
serve as a foundation for various unusual

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.790
offshoots.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:06.570
Already it is becoming more sedentary — a
lifestyle that will continue until it reaches

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.950
truly extreme levels…

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:15.340
In the forests of what is now Eastern Africa,
another major tree climbing species has emerged

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:16.890
in the Lanky Frogget.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:22.349
While they still spend some time on the forest
floor, these thin, long-legged organisms are

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:26.850
expert climbers, patiently navigating from
branch to branch.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:32.050
Even more than the Snapapple, the Froggets
descendants will be the champions of the world’s

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.050
canopies.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:38.010
And the last major niche filled is that of
the grazing herbivore, which is currently

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.750
occupied by the Lowland Dogus.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.480
These heard dwelling lifeforms are cousins
of the Greater Moistboy, and have similarly

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.670
made the critical advancement of raising their
frame off the ground.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:54.880
A wide range of future herbivorous megafauna
will owe their evolutionary ancestry to the

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:01.910
Dogus — as the various Eofrog descendants
continue to diversify and expand in number…

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:06.780
On the dry plains of the Cretaceous period,
a mighty predator comes to rule.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.709
The Bullpronks are the descendants of the
Greater Moistboy — and have become efficient

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:12.930
pack predators.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:19.370
They hunt grazing animals in coordinated prides,
much like lions in our modern day.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:24.750
To communicate while on patrol, Bullpronks
can raise and lower their tails like signal

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.650
flags, and even flush them with color.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:32.910
These tails are actually a retained trait
from their tadpole stage, showing these lifeforms

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:39.370
have had to get creative to take over niches
typically unassociated with amphibians.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.680
And speaking of getting creative, in the misty
rainforests of the north, the Mottled Suckerlump

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.580
is a creature whose specialized lifestyle
has caused a tremendous shift in its body

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.680
plan.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.870
Even more than their ancestors — the Marbled
Snapples — these animals are incredibly

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:00.840
inactive, with some spending their entire
adult life rooted to a single branch like

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.370
an amphibious barnacle.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:08.600
As with our timeline’s flatfish, their anatomy
seems absurd, yet is actually perfectly suited

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.530
for their methods of survival.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:15.340
The Suckerlump’s lifestyle requires few
calories, so they can subsist on the insects

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.000
that come into the range of their projectile
tongue.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.650
And with their characteristic mottled coloring,
they effectively hide from insects . . . until

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.000
it’s too late.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.060
And further along into the Cretaceous, an
even stranger variant of the Suckerlump will

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.320
emerge under the waves.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.260
The Tropical Frondle can be found nestled
in the underwater shallows, and has a truly

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.180
bizarre lifestyle.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.449
Beginning as a free-swimming tadpole, once
they mature, they never develop limbs, becoming

00:05:44.449 --> 00:05:47.210
completely stationary filter feeders.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.620
This lifestyle requires little brain power,
so like soft bodied ‘sea squirts’ of our

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:58.600
timeline, Tropical Frondle shockingly digest
most of their brains upon reaching adulthood

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.620
— repurposing the valuable proteins elsewhere.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.140
The Frondle’s most characteristic adult
feature is their highly derived tongue, which

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:11.630
has evolved sticky branches to catch the planktonic
organisms it survives on.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:18.450
At this point in the timeline, some creatures
have clearly become unrecognizable as amphibians…

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:24.010
And high in the treetops around the same period,
a frog with an equally bizarre body plan makes

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:25.030
its home.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.230
The Tree Frixel is a descendent of the Lanky
Frogget, the other tree-dwelling specialist

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.759
that emerged alongside the Snapapple.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:37.870
The Tree Frixel is even better adapted for
the trees however, with a body plan that at

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:43.190
first seems implausibly awkward, but makes
perfect sense when the Tree Frixel is in its

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.270
element.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:49.680
Like our primates, Tree Fixels swing through
the canopy above — with their hindlimbs

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.940
perfectly positioned to navigate from branch
to branch, as this biomechanical visualization

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:55.940
illustrates.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:01.669
As a result, the Tree Frixels have developed
thumb-like digits, and a general rise in cognitive

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:02.729
ability.

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:07.699
Given time, they may give rise to something
truly exciting…

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:12.850
One relative of the Frixel will take a very
different path than its tree climbing cousins.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:18.110
In the forests of our timeline’s Central
America, you might spot a Gliding Alloo.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:23.520
Thus far in the Amphiterran timeline, amphibians
have yet to conquer the skies in the way our

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.669
birds or bats have, as their ectothermic,
low metabolism bodies aren’t well equipped

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:31.949
for the rigors of powered flight.

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:34.130
But the Aloo has found a loophole.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:39.139
To hunt, it climbs up trees and uses eyes
rotated almost to the back of its head to

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:41.830
calculate the distance of its prey.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:47.639
Once a target is locked, the Alloo leaps back-first
towards the forest floor — using primitive

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:52.830
wings, and a specialized sac that inflates
to provide drag to slow its descent.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:58.270
As bizarre as the Alloo’s strategy might
seem, it is not unlike the early dinosaurs,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.960
Archeopteryx in our timeline, that achieved
flight.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:09.180
The future may yet hold skies dominated by
the Alloo’s curiously inverted descendants.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:14.550
Speaking of curious descendants, at the very
end of the cretaceous period, the arid grasslands

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:19.880
will give rise to a creature so bizarre it’s
at first difficult to comprehend.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:25.160
This is an Inhabited Lorge — a slow, bulky
herbivore that is the distant descendant of

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.380
the Lowland Dogus.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:31.610
At a glance, the slow-moving Lorge might seem
vulnerable to predation.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.820
But the Lorge has its own private security
in the form of the Colonial Gobolins — a

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.919
species that have evolved to live within the
Lorge’s hump.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:46.180
The tiny Gobolins are actually another branch
of the Banded Suckerlump’s family tree,

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:50.970
and they have developed a close symbiotic
relationship with their living nests.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:56.700
At the first sign of danger, the Gobolin horde
will exit the Lorge, and swarm the attacker

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.050
until it backs off.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:03.080
It’s an effective strategy, yet this highly
specialized pairing won’t have many offshoots

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.410
going forward in a changing world…

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:10.500
Yet another descendant of the Lowland Dogus
will continue on.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:16.120
Upon the cold tundra of the early Tertiary
period, one amphibian has found an ingenious

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.140
way to survive the cold.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:23.060
The Foaming Squander lives farther north than
most amphibian megafauna can exist.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:27.880
Yet the Squander stays warm thanks to its
eponymous foam.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.740
Specialized pores on the squander’s back
produce a thick, towering covering of insulating

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:33.740
foam.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:37.900
This foam is similar to the mucus coating
some frogs in our timeline produce to keep

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:39.410
their skin moist.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:44.460
The Squander’s foam is unique, however,
as it traps heat within its structure, allowing

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:47.220
the Squander to roam the polar reaches.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:51.600
With this clever adaptation, descendants of
the Squander will be well equipped for the

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.520
cooling climate...

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.190
To the south, another species that will have
many successful descendants dominates the

00:09:58.190 --> 00:09:59.680
dry steppe.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.720
The Steppe Fraggon is a carnivorous behemoth
distantly related to the nimble pack hunting

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.420
Plains Bullpronk.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:12.230
Now highly territorial, the massive Steppe
Fraggon has no need for a pack, hunting all

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:13.690
on its own.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:18.560
But the Steppe Fraggon is actually an omnivore,
with a lifestyle that could be compared to

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.020
a bear, gorging itself to build fat for a
winter hibernation.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:29.240
As the planet continues to cool, the Fraggons
will endure, and will diversify in unexpected

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.240
ways...

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.459
But one creature whose fate is more mysterious
inhabits the trees of temperate environments.

00:10:36.459 --> 00:10:41.890
High in the canopy, the astonishing Freeple
live in elevated, wooden villages.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:47.560
A descendant of the Tree Frixel, Freeple have
achieved a level of intelligence roughly analogous

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:48.980
to a human.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:53.800
Like male frigate birds, under their chin,
Freeple have a highly developed gular sac

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.300
for communication.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.430
But Freeple communication is highly complex,
with their language almost comparable to a

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.000
curious form of morse code.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.060
Even more curiously, it seems the Freeple
vanish from the Amphiterran timeline several

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.480
million years after their evolution.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.149
What fate befell these enigmatic creatures?

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:18.180
Did they die out, or given time, reach a level
of advancement that allowed for a different

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.290
kind of exit?

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:24.070
We have no way to say for sure, but no matter
the nature of their departure, the Freeple’s

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:28.160
absence makes room for even stranger species.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:34.330
By the late Tertiary, an isolated island environment
has given rise to a stunning lifeform.

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:40.300
The Splendid Fraggon is a descendant of the
Steppe Fragon and has become a peaceful herbivore,

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:45.370
spending its day wading through swampy jungles
on graceful, stilt-like legs.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:50.220
With a vibrant coloring, a Splendid Fraggon
silently picking their way through their island

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:53.100
home would likely be a magnificent sight.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:59.010
They feed on mushrooms that grow in this region
in abundance, forgoing the omnivorous lifestyle

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.040
of their ancestor…

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.600
On the mainland, however, another Fraggon
descendant has taken the opposite path.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:12.670
The Catastrophic Fraggon is the largest land-dwelling
carnivore the world of Amphiterra has ever

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:13.670
seen.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:18.130
The Catastrophic Fraggon’s rotund anatomy
might seems surprising at a glance, as it

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:21.240
almost looks too bulky to be able to stand.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:26.000
Yet examining the animal’s skeleton, the
Fragon’s center of gravity is a clavicular

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.020
ring that began overdeveloping all the way
back in the Greater Moistboy.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.630
This hypertrophied ring acts as the lynchpin
to the fraggon’s massive bulk.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:39.279
While the catastrophic fraggon’s reign is
amazing, it’s also rather short.

00:12:39.279 --> 00:12:45.370
An impending drop in temperature will cause
the age of the Fraggon to end prematurely.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:49.680
Yet one type of Fraggon, already well adapted
to the cold, will live on.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.709
Stalking the newly formed tundra, the Arctic
Fraggon thrives late into the Tertiary, protected

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:57.560
from the cold thanks to layers of blubber
and a thick hide.

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:04.040
Its most unique adaptation to stay warm, however,
is the thick coating of ‘fur’ on its underside.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:09.269
These hairs are actually dermal papillae:
tubes of skin and fatty tissue that trap warm

00:13:09.269 --> 00:13:12.050
air against the Fraggon’s body.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:18.180
And since the Amphiterran ice age is imagined
to be less severe than our own, the Fraggons

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:22.470
have all they need to continue to thrive.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.720
But there are other ways of surviving the
cold.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:30.730
A short distance south, the Eon Toad are a
species with a very different method of enduring

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:31.970
the long winter months.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:36.089
Not a type of Fraggon, the Eon Toad is actually
a descendant of the Foaming Squander that

00:13:36.089 --> 00:13:39.500
has traded its foaming coat for something
even stranger.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:45.440
Eon Toads enter a state of hibernation that
can last multiple years, enduring periods

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:50.990
of minor freezing and unfreezing as it waits
for the perfect spring to become active again.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:55.740
While this type of cold protection has never
been seen in an animal, some plants contain

00:13:55.740 --> 00:14:00.410
cryoprotectors that stop their cells from
lethal shrinkage while they allow themselves

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:03.130
to be frozen solid for long periods.

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:08.810
And on the subject of plants, the Eon toad
will often wake to find many types of vegetation

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:13.050
have grown over its body, which can be harmful
if left unchecked...

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:19.360
And in the same region, the final species
of this timeline can be found — which serve

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.560
as an example of just how alien the fauna
of Amphiterra can be.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:29.360
The Foaming Trogglefolk are another descendant
of the Foaming Squander, which are quite a

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.300
bit smaller than their ancestors.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:37.430
Incredibly, Trogglefolk live in villages constructed
from their characteristic foam, which freeze

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.829
into solid structures due to the cold.

00:14:39.829 --> 00:14:44.380
They’re also highly communal, sculpting
the foam on the bodies of family members into

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:46.680
regionally distinct ‘styles.’

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.019
This social grooming behavior is not unlike
that of certain primates in our timeline,

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:53.779
and shows an emerging intelligence.

00:14:53.779 --> 00:14:59.139
Indeed, certain communities of Trogglefolk
have begun cultivating edible fungus within

00:14:59.139 --> 00:15:03.019
their nests, demonstrating the beginnings
of agriculture.

00:15:03.019 --> 00:15:08.820
Like the Freeple before them, Trogglefolks
may rise to incredible heights…

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:14.060
Over the course of this journey, we’ve seen
pseudo-frogs rise and fall, evolve incredible

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:17.590
adaptations and fulfill unexpected niches.

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:23.029
But now, we must return to our own timeline,
where frogs, though not quite as abundant,

00:15:23.029 --> 00:15:24.820
are still quite wonderful.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:29.820
If you enjoyed learning about Amphiterra as
much as I did, you can support Roxy Valdez

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:33.649
on Patreon and social media using the links
in the description.

00:15:33.649 --> 00:15:36.860
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:42.240
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:45.670
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:54.019
See you in the next video.

